          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Form the highest five- or six-card combination and call to win the pot.',
          gameplay: '1. **Turn**: Draw one (stock or discard top), discard one. Hand stays at 5 cards until a call. 2. **Call**: With a winning combination after drawing, place it face up. Five-card: discard your 6th card. Six-card: no discard; next player draws from stock or your discard. 3. **Round completion**: If caller is not the dealer, play continues so each player up to and including dealer gets one turn. 4. **Later calls**: Others may call if they equal or beat the best shown. 5. **Winner**: Highest combination wins the pot. Equal hands split the pot (or first-caller wins in some variants).',
          keyRules: [
            'Turn-up card sets the wild rank for that deal.',
            'Winning combinations: 5 or 6 cards. No value for mixed-suit straights or non-sequence flushes.',
            "Paiute (Three Pairs) can only be called on a player's first draw (first round).",
            'If stock runs out, shuffle discard (except top) to form new stock.',
            'Pick from discard = must discard a different card; pick from stock = may discard the drawn card.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: 'Track the wild rank and build toward Five of a Kind or Four/Two. Avoid discarding cards that help opponents.',
          intermediate: 'Consider calling early with a strong hand vs waiting to improve; balance pot odds with risk of others improving.',
          advanced: 'Model opponent draws; time calls to maximize value when dealer has weak holdings.',
          tips: [
            "Paiute (Three Pairs) is a strong first-draw call; don't pass it if you have it.",
            'With wilds, Four/Two and Five of a Kind are realistic; prioritize those.',
            'Straights and flushes (mixed suit) have no valueâ€”discard them.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
